---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { url } from '../lib/url.js';
import { CONTACT, COACHES } from '../lib/site.js';

/* An application is an email, not a form post. The site is static files with no
   database and no session, and it should stay that way: there is nothing here for
   a bot to sign up to, no password to lose, and no applicant record sitting on a
   server waiting to be breached. The details below go into a mailbox, get read by
   a human, and are deleted once the decision is made.

   Prefilled so an applicant does not have to guess what we need, and so a reply
   that is missing half of it is obviously missing half of it. */
const FIELDS = [
  'Name:',
  'Governing body (BIS, SkateUK, ISU, other):',
  'Coaching licence or membership number, if you hold one:',
  'Qualification, and roughly when you took it:',
  'Rink or club, now or previously:',
  'Role: coach / judge / technical official / other:',
  'Anyone already on the guide who can vouch for you (optional):',
  'Anything else that helps us place you:',
];
const body = [
  "I'd like to help check the guide.",
  '',
  ...FIELDS,
  '',
  '— sent from figureskating.guide/coaches/',
].join('\n');
const mailto = `mailto:${COACHES}` +
  `?subject=${encodeURIComponent('Coach verification — application')}` +
  `&body=${encodeURIComponent(body)}`;
---
<Base title="For coaches" subtitle="How coaches, judges and officials can verify the guide, and how to ask to be added.">
  <h1>For coaches and officials</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Everything on this site is unverified until somebody who teaches or judges this
    for a living says otherwise. If that is you, this page is the way in.
  </p>

  <h2>What we are asking of you</h2>
  <p>
    Read a description and say whether it is right. That is the whole job. There is
    no minimum, no rota and no commitment: sign off one passage on the bus home and
    that is one passage better than it was.
  </p>
  <p>
    Sign-off attaches to a <b>passage</b>, not to a page. The same description of a
    forward outside three turn serves every page that needs it, so checking it once
    fixes it everywhere rather than putting the same paragraph in front of you eight
    times with the feet swapped. A passage carries a tick once three people have
    approved it, and each tick records who approved it and in what capacity — a
    count on its own tells a reader nothing.
  </p>
  <p>
    An objection is not a vote against two approvals. If one person says a passage is
    wrong, the tick comes off immediately and it goes back into the queue with the
    objection attached. Getting it wrong in public is the thing this project is most
    worried about; a majority is no comfort at all if the minority is right.
  </p>

  <h2>Who can take part</h2>
  <p>
    Coaches, judges and technical officials, whether you are working now or not. A
    licence is permission to teach, not evidence of knowledge, and a coach who
    stopped taking lessons five years ago has forgotten none of it — often they have
    more time to give than the people still on the ice at six in the morning. So any
    of the following is enough:
  </p>
  <ul class="plain">
    <li>
      <b>A current coaching licence</b>
      <span>British Ice Skating or another recognised body — send the number and we will check it</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>A lapsed licence, or a qualification you still hold</b>
      <span>retired, on a break, or simply not renewing — the knowledge did not lapse with it</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>A judging or technical official appointment</b>
      <span>serving or retired; you have read more of these elements than almost anyone</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>A vouch from someone already verified here</b>
      <span>for anyone whose paperwork does not fit the rows above — name them and we will ask</span>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How to apply</h2>
  <p>
    There is no sign-up form and no account to create, deliberately. You send an
    email, a human reads it, and if it checks out you are added to the list. That
    keeps the site as a set of static files with nothing to break into, and it means
    a bot cannot get in by filling something in faster than you can.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href={mailto}>Open a pre-filled email to {COACHES}</a>, or write to that
    address yourself and include:
  </p>
  <div class="derived">
    <p>{FIELDS.join(' · ')}</p>
  </div>
  <p>
    We check licence numbers against the governing body's own public register. If
    yours is lapsed or you are applying on a qualification or a vouch, say so plainly
    rather than leaving the field blank — an honest gap is easy to deal with and an
    unexplained one is not.
  </p>

  <h2>What happens to your details</h2>
  <p>
    They go to a mailbox and no further. Nothing you send is stored in the site, in a
    database or in a third party's form service, and once a decision is made the
    application email is deleted. What survives is a line saying that a named person,
    in a named capacity, approved a particular passage — which is the part a reader
    needs to see. If you would rather be shown by role than by name, say so and that
    is how it will appear.
  </p>

  <h2>Comments</h2>
  <p>
    Comment threads under a passage are for verified coaches and officials only. It
    is a small enough field that a comment carries weight because of who wrote it,
    and opening it to everyone would turn that into a comment section. Everyone else
    is very welcome to send a correction — <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT}`}>{CONTACT}</a>{' '}
    — and plenty of the fixes so far have come in exactly that way.
  </p>

  <p style="margin-top:1.5rem">
    <a href={url('about/')}>More about the guide</a>, including what it costs and what
    it deliberately is not.
  </p>
</Base>
